course: dictionary
lesson: 2436
title: Daft and friendly words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Daft means silly or foolish. It is informal, and you will hear it most in British, Irish, Australian and New Zealand speech. When a friend says «Don't be daft!», they mean "that's silly", and they usually say it with a smile.
explain: Daft has two other meanings. Daft about something means very keen on it, as in «She's daft about horses.» A daft idea is one that does not make sense, so you might hear a plan called daft if it would never work.
explain: Read the clues around the word. If the sentence is friendly and someone is laughing, daft is probably gentle, not cruel. If you are not sure, swap in "silly" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Daft means silly or foolish.
fact: «Don't be daft» means "that's silly", and it is often said in a friendly way.
fact: Daft about something means very keen on it.
fact: A daft idea is an idea that does not make sense.
fact: Daft is informal, so you would use it when chatting with friends, not usually in a formal essay.
exercise: Mum says «Don't be daft!» when you say you will fly to school. What does she mean? | Hurry up and get dressed | That's a very good plan | *That's silly
exercise: «She's daft about horses.» What does daft about mean here? | Scared of horses | *Very keen on horses | Bored by horses
exercise: Which of these is a daft idea? | A trip to the library | A picnic on a sunny day | *Trying to wash a car by sitting in a bath
test: «They argue, then laugh at how daft it is, and leave a gap by the door.» What does daft mean here? | Very tall | Sad | Quiet | *Silly
test: In Northern Ireland, «catch yourself on» means stop being daft. If someone says it to you, what are they telling you? | Hurry up | Put your coat on | *Stop being silly | Go to bed early
exam: Donna from Newcastle says «divvent be daft». What does she mean? | Don't be quiet | *Don't be silly | Don't be angry | Don't be late
